Configuring the Robot with Drag-and-Drop
Use drag-and-drop to quickly and easily configure the operations that the PickAndPlace robot can execute.
Remarks
To use drag-and-drop, drag the PickAndPlace robot over the Conveyor, the Track, or the TwoLaneTrack and
select the operation that is to take place:
Note
You can only configure the PickAndPlace robot with drag-and- drop for length-oriented objects for which
you can define sensors.
The title bar of the dialog shows the path of the PickAndPlace robot that is to move the part and of the
length-oriented object.
A complete configuration requires two drag-and-drop operations, one for picking or unloading and one for
placing or loading.
You can select one of these settings:
• Pick part
The PickAndPlace robot picks parts from a length-oriented object at a sensor. Plant Simulation creates
a front-triggered sensor with light barrier mode at the desired position on the length-oriented object
with a sensor control for moving the part to the PickAndPlace robot.
• Unload part
The PickAndPlace robot unloads parts from a Container or a Transporter at a sensor on a length-oriented
object.
Plant Simulation creates a front-triggered sensor with light barrier mode at the desired position on the
length-oriented object with a sensor control for unloading the part to the PickAndPlace robot.
• Place part
The PickAndPlace robot places parts onto a length-oriented object at a sensor. Plant Simulation creates
a rear-triggered sensor with light barrier mode at the desired position on the length-oriented object
Configuring the Robot with Drag-and-Drop
11-1872 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

without a sensor control. It also sets the settings Target Selection, Target Object, and Target Sensor
ID of the exit behavior of the PickAndPlace robot.
• Load part
The PickAndPlace robot loads parts onto a Container or a Transporter located at a sensor of a length-
oriented object.
Plant Simulation creates a sensor, which is triggered by the booking point, at the desired/clicked
position on the length-oriented object without a sensor control. It also sets the settings Target
Selection, Target Object, and Target Sensor ID of the exit behavior of the PickAndPlace robot.
Plant Simulation stops the Container or Transporter automatically at the sensor position and starts it
again automatically when it is full. If you do not want to use the default behavior, you have to create a
sensor control in which you have to make sure to start and stop the Container or Transporter.
For all of the cases described above Plant Simulation creates an entry each in the Angles Table and in the
Times Table of the PickAndPlace robot.
See also
Pick and Place Parts with the PickAndPlace Robot
