Configuring the Robot
Configuring the Robot
You can set how the arm of the robot will be animated on the tab Robot Arm Animation. The robot arm
animation paths determine which points the robot touches before it picks a part up or deposits it.
Remarks
This way you can model more specific gripping actions than the predefined ones as well as prevent
collisions manually. Robot arm animation paths are only used for robots which have at least three axes.
You can choose to animate the robot with one axis, with three axes, with four axes including a ball joint,
with five axes, or with six axes. You can, for example, see the number of axes by opening the dialog
Graphic Structure of the object and counting how often you can expand an entry with an animatable
object until no additional nested entry exists. If a robot in the structure has a different number of axes, the
next lower level applies, a robot with two axes, is for example, treated as a one axis robot.
For the animation the PickAndPlace robot expects that the required nesting of axes with animation data is
defined in the required depth as well as a suitable axes configuration.
Configuring the Robot
Plant Simulation Help 11-1873
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

The text box Animation Object on the tab MU Animation contains the defined animation objects for the
respective robot.
For a six axis robot the Animation Object looks like this:
Note
Plant Simulation automatically adjusts a model created in previous versions of Plant Simulation.
The PickAndPlace robot starts with the highest animation level and then checks one after the other from
top to bottom. Once it recognized a robot type, it does not proceed to find the next robot type.
The PickAndPlace robot applies the animation procedures in the sequence described below. It checks for a
• Six Axis Robot
• Five Axis Robot
• Four Axis Robot with Ball Joint Gripper
• SCARA Robot with Concluding Revolute Joint
• SCARA Robot with Concluding Prismatic Joint
• Three Axis Robot
• One Axis Robot
• Linear Robot
If the recognition process does not recognize any of the mentioned robot types, Plant Simulation rotates
the robot completely around the rotation axis [0,0,-1] through the Rotation Center [0,0,0].
Configuring the Robot
11-1874 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Note
The default PickAndPlace robot in versions before Plant Simulation 13.0 was a one axis robot that
rotated as such. When loading a model from previous versions, which contains such a robot, this robot
will not be changed.
You can edit the dimension of the graphic of the objects by clicking Show Manipulators on the Edit
ribbon tab or by pressing M on the keyboard.
The CranesAndMore library provides a seven axis robot moving on rails as a demonstrator.
Note
You can transform any material flow object or fluid object into a robot by exchanging its graphic with
one of the robots in the folder 3D\s3d-graphics in the Plant Simulation installation folder.
