Five Axis Robot
Plant Simulation recognizes a robot as a five axis robot/5-axis robot if exactly the specified criteria are
met.
• The first animatable object has a revolute joint around the rotation axis [0,0,1] or [0,0,-1] through the
rotation center [0,0,0].
• And the first animatable object in the next lower level has a revolute joint around the rotation axis
[0,1,0] or [0,-1,0] through the rotation center [0,0,0].
• And the first animatable object in the next lower level has a revolute joint around the rotation axis
[0,1,0] or [0,-1,0] through the rotation center [0,0,0].
• And the first animatable object in the next lower level has a revolute joint around the rotation axis
[0,1,0] or [0,-1,0] through the rotation center [0,0,0].
• And the first animatable object in the next lower level has a revolute joint around the rotation axis
[0,0,1] or [0,0,-1] through the rotation center [0,0,0].
• And the attribute _3D.AnimationObject exactly references the respective lowest animatable object.
• And a suitable animation path exists for the object, which the attribute _3D.AnimationObject
references.
Note
If any of the criteria listed above are not met, Plant Simulation will not recognize the object as a five axis
robot.
The five axis robot can reach all points in its gripping range. It can position the MU in most of the
established, but not in all positions.
Note
If you change the rotation of any animatable object, except for the fourth object, so that the object is
rotated by an angle unequal to 0° around an axis that is not the rotation axis as specified above, it might
happen that the robot cannot position or rotate the MU as expected. The same might happen, if the
fourth object is rotated differently than either by an angle of -90° around [0,1,0] or by an angle of 90°
around [0,-1,0].
This also affects the pose/joint tool 
 , which might be positioned incorrectly.
