Three Axis Robot
Plant Simulation recognizes a robot as a three axis robot/3-axis robot if exactly the specified criteria are
met.
• The first animatable object has a revolute joint around the rotation axis [0,0,1] or [0,0,-1] through the
rotation center [0,0,0].
• And the first animatable object in the next lower level has a revolute joint around the rotation axis
[0,1,0] or [0,-1,0] through the rotation center [0,0,0].
• And the first animatable object in the next lower level has a revolute joint around the rotation axis
[0,1,0] or [0,-1,0] through the rotation center [0,0,0].
• And the attribute _3D.AnimationObject exactly references the respective lowest animatable object.
• And a suitable animation path exists on the object which the attribute _3D.AnimationObject references.
Note
If any of the criteria listed above are not met, Plant Simulation will not recognize the object as a three
axis robot.
A three axis robot can reach all points within it gripping range. The MU is attached rigidly at the gripper
and is only rotated by the robot movement, not independently by itself.
Note
If you change the rotation of any animatable object, so that the object is rotated by an angle unequal to
0° around an axis that is not the rotation axis as specified above, it might happen that the robot cannot
position or rotate the MU as expected.
This also affects the pose/joint tool 
 , which might be positioned incorrectly.
Note
This robot uses user-defined robot arm animations. If the robot does not have user-defined robot arm
animations, it moves up and down to make the animation look more realistic.
Note
All robots delivered with Plant Simulation 13.0 are three axis robots. When loading a model created in
this version, the robot will not be adjusted.
The text box Animation Object on the MU Animation contains the defined animation objects for the
respective robot.
Back to Configuring the Robot
Three Axis Robot
11-1886 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

One Axis Robot
Plant Simulation recognizes a robot as a one axis robot/1-axis robot if exactly the specified criteria are
met.
• The first animatable object has a revolute joint around the rotation axis [0,0,1] or [0,0,-1] through the
rotation center [0,0,0].
• And the attribute _3D.AnimationObject exactly references the respective lowest animatable object.
• And a suitable animation path exists for the object, which the attribute _3D.AnimationObject
references.
Note
If any of the criteria listed above are not met, Plant Simulation will not recognize the object as a one
axis robot.
If Plant Simulation does not recognize any robot type, it will rotate the entire robot, which can look
similar to a one axis robot.
The one axis robot cannot reach all points within it gripping range. The MU is attached rigidly at the
gripper and is only rotated by the robot movement, not independently by itself.
Note
The one axis robot does not use any user-defined robot arm animations.
The text box Animation Object on the tab MU Animation contains the defined animation objects for the
respective robot.
Back to Configuring the Robot
One Axis Robot
Plant Simulation Help 11-1887
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Linear Robot
Plant Simulation recognizes a robot as a linear robot if exactly the specified criteria are met.
• The first animatable object has a prismatic joint.
• And the first animatable object in the next lower level has a prismatic joint with a translation direction
that is orthogonal to the one from above.
• And the first animatable object in the next lower level has a prismatic joint with a translation direction
that is orthogonal to both above.
• And the attribute _3D.AnimationObject exactly references the respective lowest animatable object.
• And a suitable animation path exists on the object which the attribute _3D.AnimationObject references.
Note
If any of the criteria listed above are not met, Plant Simulation will not recognize the object as a linear
robot.
Plant Simulation still recognizes a linear robot if the latter animatable object does not exist.
A linear robot can reach all points within it gripping range, except if the last described animatable object
does not exist. The MU is attached rigidly at the gripper and is not rotated..
Note
If you change the rotation of any animatable object, so that the object is rotated by an angle unequal to
0° around an axis that is not the rotation axis as specified above, it might happen that the robot cannot
position or rotate the MU as expected.
This also affects the pose/joint tool 
 , which might be positioned incorrectly.
The text box Animation Object on the MU Animation contains the defined animation objects for the
respective robot.
