Pull Control [PickAndPlace]
Modifies the built-in behavior of the object. The object calls the Pull Control whenever the PickAndPlace
robot is ready to pick up a new part or when it is ready when a new MU is waiting at its entrance.
Remarks
In the Pull Control you can determine which of the parts, which intend to move onto the object, the
PickAndPlace robot will accept. For this you have to get the Forward Blocking List of the object with the
method fwBlockList and unblock a MU in the list with the method unblock.
If you select Load Part onto MU at Sensor as the Target Selection, Plant Simulation only considers the
PickAndPlace robot to be ready if a Container/Transporter is waiting at the sensor. This also applies if you
programmed a Pull Control. It will only be called when a Container/Transporter is waiting at the sensor.
Note
If you only need the first entry of the Forward Blocking List, you can use the read-only-attribute
FwBlockListEntry1 for those objects which have a Forward Blocking List. Access is much faster than
with the method fwBlockList.
Let us assume that red parts are especially important and thus have a priority for being processed. Then
you can always pull red parts first from the predecessors and only then pull parts in other colors.
Note
The Pull Control does not determine if a MU is to be moved to this or to another successor. Instead, it
selects one of the parts which have already been determined to move on to this successor.
Select the Path to an Existing Method
Click the ellipsis button. Navigate to the location of the Method in the dialog Select Object [for controls]
and click OK. This inserts the name of the Method into the text box of the Control.
Press F2 in the text box to open the Method. Then type in the source code of the Control.
Pull Control [PickAndPlace]
